    "faq_hot_honey_king": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_honey": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Which hot honey should I use?",
            "text": "<p>Kayla uses a bottled hot honey, and any chili-infused honey from the grocery store will do. To make your own, warm &frac14; cup of honey with &frac12; teaspoon of red chili flakes and a pinch of salt for two minutes, then let it sit off the heat for ten. Because the honey is doing the caramelizing here, a runnier one spreads better than a thick, crystallized jar.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_method": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Air fryer or oven &mdash; does it matter?",
            "text": "<p>The air fryer gets you deeper caramelization in less time: <strong>8&ndash;10 minutes at 400&deg;F</strong> versus <strong>12&ndash;14 minutes</strong> in the oven at the same temperature. The oven is more forgiving and easier when you are cooking four portions. On a grill, hold the honey until the last two minutes or the sugar will scorch. See the comparison table above.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_temp": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What internal temperature should King Salmon hit?",
            "text": "<p>Pull it at about <strong>130&deg;F</strong> at the thickest point. Carryover heat carries it up while it rests for 3&ndash;5 minutes, which leaves a King Salmon fillet silky rather than chalky &mdash; and this is a high-fat fish, so there is real room for error. Full detail in our <a href=\"/pages/the-ultimate-guide-to-salmon-internal-temp\" title=\"The Ultimate Guide to Salmon Internal Temp\">guide to salmon internal temp</a>.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_potato": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What is a Japanese sweet potato, and can I substitute?",
            "text": "<p>Purple-skinned with pale gold flesh, drier and nuttier than the orange kind, and it crisps at the edges instead of going soft. A regular orange sweet potato works &mdash; cut the rounds a little thicker and expect a wetter, sweeter result. Delicata squash or baby potatoes both slot in cleanly too.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_greens": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Will the spinach wilt into a mess?",
            "text": "<p>Only in the good way. Lay the spinach down cold and undressed and set the hot salmon directly on top &mdash; it collapses just enough while the warm honey and the salmon fat run down and dress it. If you would rather keep the greens fully crisp, use arugula instead &mdash; that is what Kayla builds hers on in the video, with shaved parmesan and a few slices of avocado alongside.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_defrost": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I defrost Seatopia King Salmon?",
            "text": "<p>Best result: move it to the refrigerator the night before, still wrapped, and let it thaw slowly. If you forgot, take it out about an hour before you cook, or drop the vacuum-sealed portion into a bowl of cold water for 30&ndash;60 minutes. Never thaw at room temperature for hours or in the microwave.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_honey",
        "q_method",
        "q_temp",
        "q_potato",
        "q_greens",
        "q_defrost"
      ],
